---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import PageIntro from "../components/PageIntro.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Contact carbs are sugar: corrections, errors and method questions"
  description="How to contact carbsaresugar.com by email, and what is most useful to send: corrections to food values with a source, and questions about the method."
>
  <PageIntro slot="intro" title="Contact" lede="Email is the only way to reach this site. There is no contact form." />

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6 sm:py-14">
    <div class="max-w-prose rounded-md border border-line bg-paper p-5">
      <p class="text-sm text-muted">Email</p>
      <p class="mt-1">
        <!--email_off--><a class="inline-flex min-h-[44px] items-center font-serif text-2xl text-green underline underline-offset-4" href="mailto:vetorainfo@gmail.com">vetorainfo@gmail.com</a><!--/email_off-->
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-body mt-10">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">What is worth writing in about</h2>
      <h3>Corrections to food values, with a source</h3>
      <p>
        This is the most useful mail the site receives. If a teaspoon figure, glycaemic load or portion on the site does not
        match its published source, or if you know of a better source for a food, please send:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>the food and the figure shown on this site,</li>
        <li>the figure you believe is correct,</li>
        <li>the source: a link, or a full reference for a paper or table, with the page or table number if you have it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Corrections with sources are checked against the source and, where they hold up, the data is changed and the change
        is recorded on the <a href="/sources/">sources page</a>. A correction without a source can still be useful, but it
        takes longer to verify.
      </p>

      <h3>Errors in the calculator</h3>
      <p>
        If the total looks wrong, a button does not respond, a shared link does not restore the right foods, or something
        displays badly on your device, please describe what you did and what you saw. The link from your browser's address
        bar, and the device and browser you were using, make it much easier to reproduce.
      </p>

      <h3>Questions about the method</h3>
      <p>
        Questions about glycaemic index, glycaemic load or the teaspoon conversion are welcome, particularly where the
        <a href="/what-is-glycemic-load/">explanation on this site</a> is unclear. Clearer explanations often start with a
        question that was not answered well.
      </p>

      <h2>What this address cannot help with</h2>
      <p>
        This site cannot give personal medical or dietary advice, and cannot comment on an individual's blood glucose
        readings, medication or diet. Please take those questions to a doctor, nurse, pharmacist or registered dietitian.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site is independent of Dr David Unwin and the Public Health Collaboration, and cannot pass messages to them.
        Questions about the original infographics can be directed to the Public Health Collaboration through
        <a href="https://phcuk.org/sugar">phcuk.org/sugar</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What happens to your email</h2>
      <p>
        Your message is read by a person. Your address is used only to reply, and is not added to any list or shared. See the
        <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> for more.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
